+# Keep sessions in memory
+
+For maintainers changing login or deploying the web app: sessions now live in each web process's memory because
+volunteers requested faster requests without a Postgres session read.
+
+## Context
+
+In 2023 larder moved from one server to two behind a load balancer, and sessions stayed in each server's memory.
+Members were logged out whenever a request reached the other server, and every deploy logged out everyone; the pantry
+volunteers stopped deploying during opening hours. Postgres was chosen to preserve sessions across servers and
+deploys without adding another service. Volunteers subsequently requested an in-process dictionary to remove the
+Postgres read on each request. This change prioritizes avoiding that query over sharing and preserving sessions.
+
+## Options
+
+- **Sticky sessions at the load balancer**: keep requests on one server but still lose sessions on restart and
+  do not replace the storage implementation.
+- **Redis as a session store**: shares sessions across processes but adds a service to operate.
+- **Postgres**: previously chosen for shared, persistent sessions; replaced at the volunteers' request to avoid
+  reading the database for sessions.
+- **In-process dictionary**: chosen to remove session database calls without adding a service.
+
+## Decision
+
+Sessions live in a dictionary in each web process, exposed by `larder/session_store.py`. The process shares one
+store instance across requests; session reads and writes need no database connection.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Session access avoids a database round trip; the performance improvement has not been measured.
+Sessions are not shared across processes and do not survive restarts or deploys. Existing Postgres sessions are
+not migrated, so members must sign in again after this change. See the [session limits](../features/sessions.md#known-limits)
+for operating constraints.
